    <statement><p>Evaluate the limit:</p>
<p><m>{\displaystyle \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{{\bf r}(t+h)-{\bf r}(t)}{h}}</m> for <m>{\bf r}(t)=\left\lt t^{-3},\sin t,1\right&gt;</m></p>
<p><m>{\bf r}^{\prime}(t)=\langle</m><fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/>,<fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0002"/>,<fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0003"/><m>\rangle</m></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-3t^{-4}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\cos\mathopen{}\left(t\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>\frac{\,d\bf r\it}{\,dt}</m>. Using componentwise differentiation yields:



<m>\lim_{h \to 0} \frac{\bf r\it(t+h)-\bf r\it(t)}{h} = \frac{\,d\bf r\it}{\,dt} = \left\lt 
  \frac{\,d}{\,dt}(t^{-3}) , \frac{\,d}{\,dt}(\sin t) ,
  \frac{\,d}{\,dt}(1) \right&gt; = \left\lt  -\frac{3}{t^{4}},\cos t,0\right&gt;</m>



</solution>
